- V2.5: Qwen loop + dynamic probe spawning (stuck detection,
  operator-provided paths/credentials/context on demand, auth
  orchestration via Qwen, full chain orchestration)
- V2.7: Knowledge vault activated as RAG ingestion source (embed
  completed investigations; ChromaDB embedded + nomic-embed-text
  via Ollama; no cloud calls)
- V2.8: RAG retrieval injection into Qwen prompts (top-K similar
  past chains included as context on every Qwen call)
- V2.9: Vault curation UI in backend admin (view/edit/delete vault
  entries, mark canonical vs deprecated)
- V3: Manual prompt refinement cadence (operator reviews aggregated
  feedback, refines system prompts, version-bumps; prompts in
  apps/conductor/prompts/qwen/, version-controlled, operator-edited)
- V4+: TBD based on what's working

**EXPLICITLY DROPPED (do not implement)**

- CVE matcher chain. Dormant infrastructure (cves table,
  cve_watchlist, nvd_sync code, validation/engines/cve_matcher.py)
  stays in DB and codebase — parked, not wired, recoverable.
  Reason for drop: reference/annotation work rather than action work;
  medium-ROI compared to enumeration depth (V1.7); not worth a
  PR cycle at this stage.

**EXPLICITLY DEFERRED (not in V1.6 or V1.7)**

- ZAP active scan — exploitation-phase work (sends payloads;
  operator-armed semantics needed first); belongs in V2 alongside
  Metasploit
- AJAX spider in ZAP — defer until regular spider proves insufficient
- Catalog mapping (finding_type → exploit category) — exploit
  categories themselves get defined in V2 exploitation design;
  mapping is downstream of that decision
- Tag-based filtering in nuclei — defer until severity filtering
  proves insufficient

## Design notes for distant work

Notes for V2.7+ planning. These do NOT need to be implemented until
that stage but are documented now so design choices made in
intervening PRs don't accidentally box them out.

- **Knowledge vault dormant code MUST NOT be deleted.** It is the
  planned ingestion target for V2.7 RAG work. Per dormant
  capabilities audit, it lives in apps/investigation_core/exports/
  and exporters/. Keep parked.
- **Conductor audit log is the RAG source of truth.** Every
  orchestrator event already carries a correlation_id per design
  doc. This is exactly what RAG needs to trace "this finding led to
  this exploit which solved this lab." Maintain this discipline.
- **Findings schema is RAG-ready.** Current fields (id, next_actions,
  evidence, related_findings, confidence) cover what
  embedding/retrieval needs. No schema changes anticipated for V2.7.
- **Local-first RAG stack.** ChromaDB embedded (no separate service),
  nomic-embed-text via Ollama (already running). No cloud calls.
  Operator owns all data.
- **Prompt versioning lives in version-controlled files.** System
  prompts go in apps/conductor/prompts/qwen/v1.md, v2.md, etc.
  Operator-edited, not auto-generated.
- **Wordlist registry pattern.** Wordlists live under data/wordlists/
  in three categories (credentials, paths, payloads). Engines request
  wordlists by ID via apps.conductor.wordlists.load_entries. New
  wordlists registered by adding to REGISTERED_WORDLISTS in
  apps/conductor/wordlists.py. Format-aware loader handles yaml, csv,
  txt transparently. Note: config/credentials/ssh_defaults.yaml and
  probe/wordlists/default-passwords.csv remain in place (referenced by
  files/ssh.py and auth_recon.py respectively — not updated in D.0).
- **Catalog mapping deferred.** New finding types (service_detected,
  vulnerability_confirmed, vulnerability_assessed,
  verbose_banner_disclosed, waf_cdn_detected,
  http_methods_advertised) currently fall into Uncategorized sidebar
  bucket. This is INTENTIONAL until exploitation phase design is
  locked. Forcing recon/enum findings into exploit-focused categories
  (Misconfig, Infrastructure Exposure, etc.) before that design is
  settled would conflate enumeration with exploitation. Uncategorized
  + catch-all cards (from PR D-fix) provide full visibility;
  categorization is a polish concern for later.
  **CONSTRAINT: The Exploitation sidebar showing "Uncategorized: N"
  (N > 0) during recon/enum-only investigations is the correct
  intermediate state. Do NOT treat this as a bug. Do NOT "fix" it
  by forcing recon/enum finding types into exploit-focused
  categories. Findings are already visible to the operator via
  catch-all cards. Categorization decisions are deferred until
  exploitation logic is designed.**
- **Uncategorized as intentional state.** The Exploitation sidebar's
  Uncategorized bucket reflects "findings exist, exploitation pathway
  not yet designed." During recon/enum-focused PRs, this bucket will
  be non-empty and that's correct. Do not force categorization of
  recon/enum findings into exploit-focused categories before
  exploitation phase design is locked — it conflates discovery with
  exploitation and locks in bad UX. Uncategorized + catch-all cards
  provide full visibility; that's enough until exploitation logic
  exists.
- **max_chain_depth tuning.** Currently set to 3 in
  _run_all_probes_via_conductor. Future PRs adding deeper chain
  trees (CVE matcher → exploit suggester → operator-armed exec)
  may need higher. Watch for cases where legitimate chains hit the
  depth cap — that's the signal to raise it. Watch for runaway
  behavior — that's the signal something's pointing back at itself.
- **Multi-gate engine pattern (PR E).** Engines that have severity
  tiers or other dimensions where visibility depends on ANY of N gates
  use `gate_ids: tuple[str, ...]` instead of single `gate_id: str`.
  The `engine_is_visible(engine)` helper in gates.py handles both.
  An engine declaring BOTH gate_id and gate_ids is fail-closed —
  this is intentional: misconfigured engines are blocked, not
  visible. Never set both attributes on an engine class.
- **Adapter responsibility boundary (PR E-fix).** Adapters are thin
  subprocess/HTTP wrappers only. They own: CLI flag construction,
  exit code check, output parsing, timeout handling, HTTP call
  construction. They do NOT own: scope/policy (which severities to
  scan, which templates to use, which scan types to run), feature
  gates, or profile systems. The engine layer owns scope and passes
  it down via config. If an adapter has hardcoded severities or
  profiles, that's a design violation. nuclei_adapter.py is the
  canonical example: engine computes active_severities from gate
  state, passes them as config["severity"]; adapter uses them
  verbatim. zap_adapter.py follows the same split: engine layer
  decides whether to call run_spider() vs run_passive_scan() based
  on gate state; adapter functions are unconditional — they run when
  called.
- **ZAP active scan deferred (PR F decision).** ZAP spider + passive
  scan are enumeration-phase work (read-only, observe traffic).
  Active scan (fuzzing, injection testing) is exploitation-phase work
  and belongs in a later PR alongside Metasploit and exploitation
  template work. Do NOT add active scan to zap_engine.py without
  explicit operator approval and a matching exploitation-phase design.
- **Three-outcome enumeration model (V1.7).** V1.7 introduces
  validation_status on findings with three values: validated (verified
  worth exploit's time), dropped (verified dead end, hidden from
  operator UI by default), incomplete (worth exploit's time but
  missing data; carries enumeration_gaps array describing what's
  missing). The "incomplete" status is the handoff point for V2.x
  Qwen collaboration — exploitation can show the gap to operator via
  Qwen, get input, retry validation.
- **Enumeration gap surfacing (NOT auto-enrichment).** When an
  enumeration rule needs a field that's absent from recon's evidence,
  the finding is marked enumeration_blocked with
  enumeration_block_reason. System surfaces aggregated gaps in admin
  UI ("Top recon gaps: passive_probe missing content_type — 18
  findings blocked"). Operator decides whether to enrich recon (small
  PR to add the field to a recon engine) or adjust the enumeration
  rule. System does NOT automatically modify recon engines or trigger
  recon re-runs. Operator drives car.
- **Filtered findings retained in DB.** Dropped findings are NOT
  deleted — they stay in normalized_findings with
  validation_status='dropped'. Hidden from operator UI default view,
  accessible via a "show filtered" toggle. Reasons: audit trail when
  filter seems too aggressive; debuggability when investigation
  behavior is unexpected; recovery if filter rules later change and
  previously-dropped findings become relevant.
- **Rule storage as Python code, not YAML.** V1.7 rules are implemented as Python functions in apps/conductor/rules/. Reasons: rule logic composes conditions and helpers cleanly in code; YAML adds loader + validator + expression evaluator complexity without operator benefit at this stage; operator does not edit rules in production (refinement happens via code-level PRs). If operator workflow later demands live rule editing, migration to YAML is viable but not required now.
- **Recon evidence is typed-list shaped, not flat-list shaped.**
  V1.7 filtering rules depend on per-item metadata (status_code,
  content_type, method, input type). api_recon hit_paths, sqli_recon
  detected_login_endpoints, and cmdi_recon form_inputs are now
  list[dict] not list[str]. Consumers that read these fields
  (aggregations.py, suggestions/probes.py) use isinstance(x, dict)
  guards for backward compat with old flat-list DB records. New
  engines and rules must write and expect the typed shape. Do not
  regress to flat lists.
- **UI design discipline for status badges (V1.7 PR J).** Per-finding
  validation_status badges: validated=green, incomplete=orange/yellow,
  dropped=grey, pending=no badge. Future statuses (e.g., V2 might add
  "exploitable" or "exploit_attempted") should extend this palette
  consistently. Keep badge text short (single word + count if applicable).
  Gap descriptions for incomplete findings use human-readable labels mapped
  from machine codes (csrf_token_needed → "CSRF token required"). Mapping
  lives in workspace.js (GAP_LABELS constant). The dominant validation_status
  for group cards (e.g., security_headers groups multiple findings) is
  computed by _dominant_validation() in aggregations.py: all-dropped →
  dropped; any-validated → validated; any-incomplete → incomplete; else pending.
- **evidence_gap rule protocol (V1.7 PR J).** Rules return
  RuleResult.evidence_gap(reason) when they would classify a finding but
  required evidence is missing. This is NOT first-match-wins — the rule chain
  continues. FilterOrchestrator records the reason in enumeration_block_reason.
  Finding stays validation_status='pending'. If a later rule classifies, both
  fields are set. evidence_gap is opt-in per rule — don't add it to every rule,
  only where the absence of a field is meaningfully actionable by the operator.
- **Pattern catalog as documentation and enforcement device (PR L).** The
  tests/conductor/_patterns/ directory serves a dual role: the README.md is the
  living catalog of architectural bug patterns (what went wrong, why, what the fix
  was); the test_seam_*.py files are the enforcement layer (each pattern has at
  least one test that would FAIL if the bug were reintroduced). Keeping them in
  tests/ rather than docs/ means the catalog is run on every test suite invocation —
  it cannot rot silently. When adding new seams (V2 prompt construction, RAG
  retrieval, exploit execution chain), add a new test_seam_*.py for that seam before
  shipping the code, not after the first bug surfaces. The pattern that caused the
  most damage is always the most recent one.
- **Factory scaffold cleanup deferred (core/ and sdk/).** The core/ and sdk/
  directories at repo root are vestigial factory scaffolding. They CANNOT be
  removed yet: sdk/ is imported throughout apps/ via "from sdk import Logger"
  (apps/validation/services/metasploit.py, apps/validation/api/*, apps/scanner_
  orchestration/*, and others); core/ is the application entrypoint (core/main.py
  → core/api/app.py). Cleanup requires a dedicated PR to replace all sdk.Logger
  usages with stdlib logging before sdk/ can be removed. Until that PR lands,
  both directories are load-bearing. Do NOT delete them assuming they are inert.
- **FilterOrchestrator runs after enumeration, not recon (PR K).** FilterOrchestrator
  was originally wired to run after the recon phase. That was wrong: recon phase only
  produces recon-engine findings (passive_probe, nmap). Chain-dispatched enumeration
  engines (sqli_recon, auth_recon, api_recon, cmdi_recon, nuclei, zap, plugins) emit
  their findings during the enumeration phase. Running FilterOrchestrator after enumeration
  ensures the complete finding pool is available for both the first pass and the
  reinvocation second pass. Do NOT move this back to after recon — the two-pass logic
  depends on a complete pool, and the validate rules for sqli/api_exposure depend on
  evidence fields that only enumeration engines produce.
- **Two-parallel-ID-systems bug (PR L).** From PR B until PR L, findings had two
  different IDs simultaneously: Conductor's deterministic fnd_xxx (16-char, generated by
  generate_finding_id()) lived in memory; NormalizedFinding in the DB held a random UUID4
  (default_factory=lambda: str(uuid4())) because _persist in routes.py never passed
  finding_id=evt.id to the constructor. FilterOrchestrator UPDATE WHERE finding_id='fnd_xxx'
  always matched 0 rows — silent failure for the entire V1.7 feature set. Eight PRs of
  work accumulated on top of it. Fix: one line in _persist — finding_id=(evt.id or
  str(uuid4())). Lesson: when data crosses between layers with their own ID generation,
  write an explicit round-trip test that proves the ID survives the crossing. The test
  must be written to FAIL against pre-fix code; a test that always passes proves nothing.
- **Seam bugs evade mock-based unit tests.** Eight bugs in V1.6–V1.7 shared the same root
  shape: mechanically valid code at each layer, silent mismatch at the seam between layers.
  Mock tests verified each layer in isolation and passed. The bugs only surfaced during live
  integration or DB inspection. Unit tests are insufficient for cross-layer contracts.
  tests/conductor/_patterns/ introduces seam tests — tests that verify data crossing between
  layers preserves identity, shape, and semantics. Patterns documented so far: Two-Parallel-
  ID-Systems, Attribution Loss at Seam, Silently-Disabling Default, Adapter Shape Mismatch,
  Dangling Reference, Resource-Class-Blind Policy, Wrong DB Target. As new architectural
  seams are introduced (V2 prompt construction, RAG retrieval, exploit execution), add new
  test_seam_*.py files to this directory.
- **files/ directory is the canonical protected backup.** files/Dockerfile,
  files/docker-compose.yml, files/pyproject.toml, files/app.py are the originals
  from 2026-05-13. They are the recovery source if build config files are ever
  replaced again. Do not modify files/ without operator direction. After any
  session involving subagent operations or broad file writes, verify the 4 build
  config files (deployment/docker/Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml, pyproject.toml,
  core/api/app.py) have not been silently reverted by comparing their sizes/content
  against the files/ backups before running docker compose build.

## Gate registry

Runtime feature gates at `apps/conductor/gates.py`. In-memory, reset on restart.
Thread-safe (threading.Lock). Admin UI toggle at /admin/settings.

| Gate ID                     | Default | Effect |
|-----------------------------|---------|--------|
| `nmap_external_scan`        | OFF     | When ON, nmap runs against EXTERNAL targets. When OFF, nmap only scans LOCAL/PRIVATE targets. |
| `ssh_credential_testing`    | OFF     | SSH default-credential validation via paramiko. |
| `telnet_credential_capture` | OFF     | Telnet cleartext-credential capture. |
| `smb_null_session_enum`     | OFF     | SMB null-session enumeration via impacket. |
| `wordpress_vuln_check`      | OFF     | WordPress vulnerability checks on HTTP services. |
| `drupal_vuln_check`         | OFF     | Drupal vulnerability checks on HTTP services. |
| `apache_misconfig_check`    | OFF     | Apache misconfiguration checks on HTTP services. |
| `nginx_misconfig_check`     | OFF     | Nginx misconfiguration checks on HTTP services. |
| `nuclei_severity_info`      | **ON**  | Run nuclei templates at info severity. |
| `nuclei_severity_low`       | **ON**  | Run nuclei templates at low severity. |
| `nuclei_severity_medium`    | OFF     | Run nuclei templates at medium severity. |
| `nuclei_severity_high`      | OFF     | Run nuclei templates at high severity. |
| `nuclei_severity_critical`  | OFF     | Run nuclei templates at critical severity. |
| `zap_passive_scan`          | OFF     | ZAP passive scan: analyze observed HTTP traffic for alerts (headers, cookies, info disclosure). Read-only, no payloads. Run after spider for best coverage. |
| `zap_spider`                | OFF     | ZAP spider: crawl target to discover URLs, forms, parameters. Generates HTTP traffic visible in access logs. |

Multi-gate engines (nuclei, zap): visible iff ANY gate in gate_ids is ON. Single-gate engines: visible iff the one gate is ON. Both gate_id and gate_ids → fail-closed.

Use `get_gate(gate_id)`, `set_gate(gate_id, bool)`, `list_gates()`, `reset_all_gates_to_defaults()`, `engine_is_visible(engine)`.

## Feature flags

- `CONDUCTOR_PARALLEL_TRACK` (env var, default `false`): when `true`,
  ALL bootstrap probes (passive_probe, sqli_recon, cmdi_recon,
  auth_recon, api_recon) dispatch via Conductor + Execution Orchestrator.
  All other probes (wellknown, fingerprint, cors) remain on the existing
  path (not yet wrapped). Will be removed after live parity gate clears
  and Conductor path becomes default.
  Toggle: set `CONDUCTOR_PARALLEL_TRACK=true` in `.env` or shell.

## Live parity gate — PASSED

Completed against PortSwigger lab. Three wiring bugs found and fixed:
1. Conductor `_run_phases()` never emitted `investigation_completed` to
   the bus (PhaseOrchestrator emitted it, Conductor did not — added
   Conductor-level emission + `investigation_failed` for exception path).
2. `_run_all_probes_via_conductor` in runtime.py never populated
   `PhasedRun.completed_at`, `recon_run`, or `enumeration_run` stubs
   (added stub PhaseRun population after `done.wait()` resolves).
3. PhaseRun stubs were created post-completion with `completed_at`
   already set, so `is_recon_active()` returned False throughout the
   entire parallel-track run (frontend polling stopped after first
   poll; finding cards never appeared until hard refresh). Fixed by
   creating `recon_run` stub with `completed_at=None` BEFORE
   `conductor.start_investigation()`, then mutating `completed_at`
   after `done.wait()` resolves.

Parity confirmed: flag OFF and flag ON produce matching findings on
real lab. Admin UI shows investigation in RECON_RUNNING during run and
disappears on completion (expected — COMPLETED is filtered from active
list). Ready for PR C.

## Operator preferences

- Senior engineer voice. Direct, concise, technically accurate.
  Push back on weak ideas.
- No git ceremony — repo has no git.
- Small fix = small prompt. Don't add scope not requested.
- No Playwright / screenshots / CLAUDE.md ceremony in prompts unless
  asked.
- Reviewer pushback is often overridden by operator — execute locked
  decisions, don't re-litigate.

## Documentation discipline (MANDATORY)

Every Claude Code prompt must end by updating relevant .md files so
future sessions have current context. The canonical files:

- CLAUDE.md — top-level project context, locked decisions, current
  status. Update when status, locked decisions, or delivery sequence
  changes.
- docs/architecture/orchestration.md — architecture design doc.
  Update when architecture decisions change.
- docs/audits/*.md — audit reports. Static once written; do not
  modify retroactively. Create new audit files when running new
  audits.

Do not create new .md files outside docs/architecture/, docs/audits/,
or repo root, without explicit operator request.

Do not modify README or CHANGELOG without explicit operator request.
